  "body": "### What should we add?\n\nCurrently, the `SparseLabelOp.register_length` property dictates that it can be `int | None`.\r\nThe idea is that operator instances do not have a fixed length, but instead treat this as a lower bound.\r\nA user may set a specific value for this length, or it is inferred from the maximum index.\r\n\r\nHowever, this latter point is currently a bit problematic, because this is done implicitly during the `_validate_keys` call, a side effect that is in an unnatural place and which does _not_ occur, when `copy=False` or `validate=False` is used during construction.\r\n\r\nFurthermore, the `register_length` attribute of operators in used in various places where it being `None` will actually cause problems. Here are some examples which (to my surprize) `mypy` does not inform us about:\r\n- https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-nature/blob/c80eb0e7e50cd7c351e1a8cc72a26ad296d006db/qiskit_nature/second_q/mappers/linear_mapper.py#L56 (similar in the `LogarithmicMapper`)\r\n- https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-nature/blob/c80eb0e7e50cd7c351e1a8cc72a26ad296d006db/qiskit_nature/second_q/operators/sparse_label_op.py#L447 (this one won't outright break, but might give false positives)\r\n- https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-nature/pull/1189#discussion_r1212752232\r\n\r\nAs a way forward, I propose the following:\r\n- remove `None` from the possible return value of `SparseLabelOp.register_length`\r\n- extract the lower bound setting from `_validate_keys` into a separate utility method (not ideal because we need to loop over the labels again. Maybe we can find a more performant solution here)\r\n- during the property getter of `register_length`, if the underlying value currently is `None`, run the utility over the labels to infer the actual lower bound (or we do this during `__init__` but I would prefer the lazy way). Once gotten, we should store this lower bound in the internal attribute to avoid rerunning this lower bound finding multiple times.",
